<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chamar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jatav</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chamars are a community of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, classified as a Scheduled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">Caste</a> in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, traditionally associated with leather tanning and shoemaking, though the historian Ramnarayan Rawat holds that this association was constructed and that they were historically agriculturists. By the late 19th century Chamars had begun to rewrite their caste histories to claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> descent. In the later 20th century the Bahujan Samaj Party, led by the Chamar leaders Kanshi Ram and Mayawati, drew on their votes. The name is now often used as a casteist slur, and the Supreme Court of India has ruled it highly offensive.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between the 1830s and the 1950s the Chamars of the United Provinces, especially around Kanpur, prospered through the British leather trade. Around 1910 U. B. S. Raghuvanshi published the Shri Chanvar Purana in Kanpur, claiming that the Chamars had once been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> rulers whose king, Chamunda Rai, was cursed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and promised a rise in the Kaliyuga after a new sage, whom Raghuvanshi identified as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>. Another section claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> as Jatavs; the Jatav Veer Mahasabha, founded in 1917, spread this claim and discriminated against lower-status Chamars such as the Guliyas.</p><p>The most influential Chamar leader of the early 20th century was Swami Achutanand, whose anti-Brahmanical Adi Hindu movement portrayed the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s original inhabitants, enslaved by Aryan invaders. In the 1940s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> promoted the Chamar politician Jagjivan Ram to counter the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>. The 1st Chamar Regiment, raised by the British on 1 March 1943, fought at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kohima">Battle of Kohima</a> and was disbanded soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chamars live mainly in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. The Jatavs, a sub-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, made up 54% of the 22,496,047 Scheduled Caste people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and Nepal's census of the same year counted 335,893 Chamars, 1.3% of the population. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> the Ad-Dharmi and the Ravidassia, who worship Guru <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidass</a>, form communities of their own: the Ad-Dharm name spread after Chamar converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> met discrimination. Chamars who moved from leatherwork to weaving took the identity of Julaha Chamar.</p><p>In villages of Rohtas and Bhojpur districts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> landlords forced women of the Chamar, Musahar and Dusadh communities into sexual relations, raped those who refused, brought false cases against their men and barred them from Rajput paths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>. Peasant organisations such as the Kisan Samiti are said to have ended these practices in the 1970s, though conflict over wages continued.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> the Ambedkarite Republican Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> remained dominated by Chamars and Jatavs, and after its decline in the 1970s the Bahujan Samaj Party drew their votes; Mayawati became Chief Minister of Uttar Pradesh. Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> communities, such as the Bhangis, complained that Chamars monopolised state benefits, while the party also brought together old rivals such as the Pasis. A large diaspora lives in the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, where Ravidassia Chamars from Doaba founded a gurdwara at Nasinu in 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chamar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the late 19th century the Criminal Tribes Act reached most untouchable castes, such as the <b>Chamars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li></ul></section>
